"Create, Connect, Control: AMD at IBC 2026 (https://www.amd.com/en/blogs/2026/create-connect-control-amd-at-ibc-2026.html)\nciteturn24search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 5 days ago; # Create, Connect, Control: AMD at IBC 2026 ... This year, the International Broadcasting Convention (IBC) will run at RAI Amsterdam from September 11 through September 14, 2026.\n\n# Create, Connect, Control: AMD at IBC 2026\n\nSep 11, 2026\n\nImage\n\n### Introduction\n\nThis year, the International Broadcasting Convention (IBC) will run at RAI Amsterdam from September 11 through September 14, 2026. The show focuses on companies that operate at any stage of the broadcast pipeline, from creation and post-production to transmission and distribution.\n\nIn my 2025 IBC blog, I wrote that AI implementations at the show had become more focused and pragmatic, with more attention paid to how the technology could be used in production. This remains true in 2026, even as AI agents — semi-autonomous AI systems that can plan and take action without requiring explicit human approval at every step — have become a much-discussed topic within the industry.\n\nPractical problem-solving is also the premise of IBC’s unique Accelerator programme which runs alongside the show’s exhibitions, speaker sessions, and meetings. The Accelerator programme unites research organizations, technical partners, and industry leaders to develop proofs of concept that address real-world problems across content creation, management, and connectivity. By connecting groups that might not otherwise find each other, IBC hopes to move new concepts towards production-ready implementations.\n\nIBC may fall in September, but work on the projects presented at the show begins much earlier. At Kickstart Day in February, teams of champions (broadcasters, studios, and research organizations) and participants (technology companies providing hardware, cloud compute, software, and engineering expertise) pitch problems they hope to solve. IBC selected nine projects for its 2026 Accelerator programme, with teams spending the following six months building proofs of concept for the fall show.\n\nAMD and HP return as joint Associate Sponsors of the IBC Accelerator Media Innovation Programme this year (AMD, for the fifth year running), and provided hardware to all of the accelerator projects. Our direct project work and my own area of focus centered on FRAMES (Federated Retrieval, Agentic Media Environment and Software (Defined Workflows)), which explores how a common semantic model and knowledge graph can connect scripts, production data, archival material, newly captured content, creative teams, and AI agents. \n### Security in the Time of Agents\n\nBut compute is only one aspect of modern media production. AMD participates in (and I sit on the board of) the Global Creative & Security Community (GC-SC), a nonprofit industry group focused on developing and sharing practices around content creation, security, governance, and emerging technologies. At IBC, GC-SC will use its September 12 security workshop to launch Collective Defense for Media & Entertainment, a threat- and intelligence-sharing program developed with the Global Resilience Federation. The goal is to give media and entertainment companies a way to share information about threats across an industry where studios, broadcasters, production companies, and technology providers increasingly depend on one another’s systems. The workshop will also cover security-focused workplace use of AI, continuous monitoring and remediation, and AI-driven threats.\n\n### See You in Amsterdam\n\nIBC has always been useful because media production forces new technologies into contact with real-world operational constraints. AI systems have to work with existing production tools. Search has to operate against actual media libraries. High-resolution footage has to move through the workstation fast enough to edit. Automation has to coexist with established control infrastructure, and increased connectivity creates security problems that individual companies cannot always solve alone.\n\nThose are the kinds of problems AMD and its partners will be focused on in Amsterdam this year. If you will be at IBC from September 11 through September 14, I hope you will stop by the Accelerator Zone, visit our partners across the show floor, and see the work for yourself.\n\n#### Article By\n\n* * *\n\nImage: Profile Image of John Canning\n\nJohn Canning\n\nDirector, Strategic Partners - Media & Entertainment and Creators\nJohn Canning is AMD's Director of Strategic Partnerships, working with key ISV partners across media, entertainment, immersive experiences and creators.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGEAK/perf_knowledge/languages/composable_kernel/knobs.md at main · AMD-AGI/GEAK · GitHub (https://github.com/AMD-AGI/GEAK/blob/main/perf_knowledge/languages/composable_kernel/knobs.md)\nciteturn24search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; `BlockGemmPipelineVersion`  | v1/v2/v3/v4/v5  | hot-loop schedule depth  | ★★★★ ...   * Prefill (large M): 256×256×64, `MPerXDL=NPerXDL=32` (test 16×16), WaveMap 4×4, v3 Intrawave, `AK1=BK1=8`.\n\n## The levers (ranked)\n\nKnob  | values  | effect  | priority\n--- | --- | --- | ---\n`MPerBlock × NPerBlock`  | 256×256, 256×128, 128×128, 128×64, 64×64  | block tile; bigger = more reuse, fewer blocks (occupancy/tail trade-off)  | ★★★★★\n`KPerBlock`  | 32, 64, 128  | K-loop tile; bigger = better MFMA/load overlap, more LDS+VGPR  | ★★★★\n`BlockGemmPipelineVersion`  | v1/v2/v3/v4/v5  | hot-loop schedule depth  | ★★★★\n`BlockGemmPipelineScheduler`  | Intrawave / Interwave  | overlap strategy  | ★★★★\n`MPerXDL × NPerXDL`  | 16×16, 32×32  | MFMA tile; 16×16 often wins on MI300X (power)  | ★★★\n`MXdlPerWave × NXdlPerWave`  | 4×4, 4×2, 2×2  | waves-per-tile map; drives VGPR & occupancy  | ★★★\n`AK1 / BK1`  | 8 (bf16), 16 (fp8)  | global-load vector width; ≥128 bit/load; must match alignment  | ★★★\n`GemmSpecialization`  | Default / MNKPadding / MNPadding  | pad guards for non-divisible shapes  | ★★\n`KBatch` (split-K)  | 1, 2, 4, 8  | atomic K split → fills CUs for small-M decode  | ★★★ (decode)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAdvancing Gaming with AMD at G2E 2026 (https://www.amd.com/en/blogs/2026/advancing-gaming-with-amd-at-g2e-2026.html)\nciteturn24search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: last week; # Advancing Gaming with AMD at G2E 2026 ... Taking place from 28 September to 1 October at The Venetian Expo, the Global Gaming Expo (G2E) is a chance to see how AMD Ryzen™ Embedded processors are helping gaming innovators deliver the next generation of casino experiences.\n\n# Advancing Gaming with AMD at G2E 2026\n\nSep 24, 2026\n\nImage\n\nTaking place from 28 September to 1 October at The Venetian Expo, the Global Gaming Expo (G2E) is a chance to see how AMD Ryzen™ Embedded processors are helping gaming innovators deliver the next generation of casino experiences. With leadership performance, advanced integrated graphics, power-efficient operation and scalable platforms with long lifecycle support, AMD Ryzen Embedded technology enables responsive, immersive multi-display systems engineered for demanding 24/7 environments, helping OEMs streamline development and bring differentiated gaming solutions to market faster.\n\nAt the show, AMD partners are showcasing the latest compute systems designed to move modern gaming forward:\n\nImage: ADLINK and AMD at G2E\n\nADLINK (Booth 2640) leads with its scalable all-in-one gaming platform powered by AMD Ryzen Embedded 8000 Series processors, delivering high performance and multi-4K display support in a compact, expandable design. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRelease Notes / GEAK (https://www.geak.ch/infos-fuer-expert